# SSH config for the agent identity's git remotes, reached via core.sshCommand in
# ~/.claude/gitconfig. Nothing else uses it — plain `ssh` is untouched.
#
# Pinning the agent for EVERY host is what makes this fail closed. Scoping the
# override to `Host github.com` protected only a remote spelled literally that way:
# an alias in ~/.ssh/config with `HostName github.com` is a different `Host` pattern,
# so it imported the personal identity and the personal 1Password agent, and pushed
# as the human account — silently, with the agent's separately revocable identity
# bypassed and the human's broader permissions in play. It cannot be repaired after
# the fact either: an option keeps the first value it is given, and `Match final`
# re-parses without clearing what the include already set, so nothing placed after
# the include can take the personal agent back off. The only ordering that holds is
# claiming the agent before the include is ever read.
#
# What survives from ~/.ssh/config is everything that is not an identity — HostName,
# User, Port, ProxyJump — so a remote elsewhere still resolves and tunnels correctly.
# What it no longer gets is the human's keys: a git remote that needs one now fails
# with a permission error, which is the loud half of the trade and the right one.
Host *
	IdentityAgent ~/.claude/run/agent.sock

Host github.com
	HostName github.com
	User git
	IdentityFile ~/.claude/claude-auth.pub
	IdentitiesOnly yes

# `Match !host` rather than a bare Include: an Include written at the end of a `Host`
# block is CONDITIONAL on that block, which would leave every non-github remote with
# no ~/.ssh/config at all — no ProxyJump, no User, no Port.
Match !host github.com
Include ~/.ssh/config
